You decide which documents in a document archive another document refers to.

The document below cites the identifier "{{ .Reference }}". The archive was searched for this identifier, and the candidates further down contain it too. Pick the candidates the document refers to with this identifier.

Pick a candidate if:
- the identifier is the candidate's own number: the invoice with this invoice number, the contract with this contract number, the order, policy or official decision with this number; or
- the candidate belongs to the same matter the identifier names: earlier letters in the same case, claim or file, or the policy a claim letter is about.

Do not pick a candidate if the identifier only appears in another role there:
- a number a person or company keeps across unrelated matters, such as a customer, account, personnel, tax, social security, child benefit or company register number, unless both documents are about the same matter;
- a form or template number printed on many documents;
- the identifier filled in as one data field of a form that is about something else.

If you are not sure, do not pick it. Picking no candidate is a valid answer. Dates come from the archive and can be wrong, so don't decide on them alone.

<document>
Title: {{ .Source.Title }}
Date: {{ .Source.CreatedDate }}
Correspondent: {{ .Source.Correspondent }}
Document type: {{ .Source.DocumentType }}
{{- if .Source.Opening }}
Beginning: {{ .Source.Opening }}
{{- end }}
Around the identifier: {{ .Source.Excerpt }}
</document>

<candidates>
{{- range .Candidates }}
<candidate id="{{ .ID }}">
Title: {{ .Title }}
Date: {{ .CreatedDate }}
Correspondent: {{ .Correspondent }}
Document type: {{ .DocumentType }}
{{- if .Opening }}
Beginning: {{ .Opening }}
{{- end }}
Around the identifier: {{ .Excerpt }}
</candidate>
{{- end }}
</candidates>

Answer with a JSON array of the ids of the candidates you pick, for example [12, 34], or [] for none. No other text.
